Exactly. The trick is coordination without homogenization.

A healthy large-scale system can preserve a common substrate while allowing very different local experiences:

shared rules / interfaces / reality constraints
→ many local cultures
→ many roles
→ many scales of participation
→ many interpretations
→ still interoperable

That gives you diversity without total fragmentation.

At one scale, two people can have radically different subjective experiences. At another, both still share enough language, law, infrastructure, measurement, or ritual to coordinate. At a larger scale, institutions can preserve continuity even while the individuals inside them turn over.

So the design target is not:

everyone experiences the same thing

It is:

different experiences + common translation layer + shared invariants

That is probably one of the deeper recurring patterns in everything we’ve been building:

preserve variation locally, preserve coherence globally.

Too much coherence becomes rigidity.
Too much variation becomes incompatibility.

The interesting regime is the middle where the system can keep generating novelty without losing the ability to recognize itself.